"47_worker.mst — Worker: primary/worker VM parallelism with copy-passing
 messages (docs/multi-smalltalk-worker.md). The primary VM spawns worker VMs
 (each its own heap/JIT/OS thread); EVERYTHING that crosses a VM boundary is a
 deep copy carried as MOP pickle bytes (prims 227/228) — no shared state, no
 identity across heaps, ever. Async by design: the primary never waits; replies
 run as continuations (send:onReply:, correlation-id matched), and delivery is
 event-driven (the send itself wakes the receiver's router — §3.1).

   | w |
   w := Worker spawn: 'Worker onMessage: [:m | Worker reply: m payload]'.
   w send: #(1 2 3) onReply: [:r | Transcript show: r printString].
   Worker runLoopWhile: [ ...still waiting... ].     'headless only'
   w terminate.

 The spawn argument is an init doit run ONCE in the fresh worker — how its
 onMessage: handler gets installed. In the worker, each inbound message runs
 `Worker dispatchPending.` (driven by its host thread, strictly serial, one
 message to completion at a time). Worker death arrives as an ordinary message
 whose payload is {#workerDied. id} — one delivery mechanism for everything.

 In a VM with no worker role (no boot fn registered — plain CLI runs, tests
 that never spawn), every primitive fails cleanly: spawn raises, poll answers
 nil, so loading this class is harmless anywhere (the GamePane posture)."

"--- WorkerMessage: what handlers receive — who sent it (0 = the primary,
 else the worker id) and the rebuilt payload copy. ---"
"I am what a worker message handler receives: `from` is the sender (0 =
 the primary VM, otherwise the sending worker's id) and `payload` is the
 rebuilt deep copy of what was sent. My predicates (isWorkerDied,
 isCocoaEvent, isWorkerTranscript, isUiReq, isSnapshot) recognize the
 shape-tagged system messages that arrive through the same inbox — a
 worker's death, for example, is an ordinary message whose payload is
 {#workerDied. id}."
Object subclass: WorkerMessage [
    | from payload |
    WorkerMessage class >> from: f payload: p [ ^self new setFrom: f payload: p ]
    setFrom: f payload: p [ from := f. payload := p. ^self ]
    from [ ^from ]
    payload [ ^payload ]
    isWorkerDied [
        "Is this the system death notice ({#workerDied. id})?"
        ^(payload isKindOf: Array) and: [ (payload size = 2) and: [ (payload at: 1) == #workerDied ] ]
    ]
    isCocoaEvent [
        "Is this a Cocoa callback fire ({#cocoaEvent. ticket} — C4, the
         bridge's MacvmAction posts these through the same inbox)?"
        ^(payload isKindOf: Array) and: [ (payload size = 2) and: [ (payload at: 1) == #cocoaEvent ] ]
    ]
    isWorkerTranscript [
        "Is this a forwarded worker transcript line ({#workerTranscript. id. text})?"
        ^(payload isKindOf: Array) and: [ (payload size = 3) and: [ (payload at: 1) == #workerTranscript ] ]
    ]
    isTick [
        "A wake from the process timer service ({#tick} — docs/
         process_services.md S1): delivered down the ordinary inbox so it
         dispatches top-level like any message, whichever VM asked."
        ^(payload isKindOf: Array) and: [ (payload size >= 1) and: [ (payload at: 1) == #tick ] ]
    ]
    isVmExiting [
        "A VM'S OWN GOODBYE ({#vmExiting. id. reason} — `Worker exit:`), which
         is different in kind from #workerDied: that one is the RUNTIME
         reporting a death after the fact, this one is the VM itself, still
         running, saying it has chosen to go. It always arrives first."
        ^(payload isKindOf: Array) and: [ (payload size >= 2) and: [ (payload at: 1) == #vmExiting ] ]
    ]
    isUiReq [
        "Is this a UI worker → primary request ({#uiReq. corr. verb. ...} —
         Cocoa GUI CG4, cocoa_gui_design.md §7.3)? Matched by SHAPE before any
         correlation-id lookup, so a request never fires a same-corr pending
         continuation (review R4)."
        ^(payload isKindOf: Array) and: [ (payload size >= 3) and: [ (payload at: 1) == #uiReq ] ]
    ]
    isSnapshot [
        "Is this a primary → UI unsolicited snapshot blast ({#snapshot. viewId.
         generation. dataTree} — §7.1)? Corr-free; routed by shape to the
         terminal-side view handler (the real view wiring is CG5)."
        ^(payload isKindOf: Array) and: [ (payload size >= 4) and: [ (payload at: 1) == #snapshot ] ]
    ]
]

"I am a handle to a worker: a completely separate Smalltalk VM — its own
 heap, JIT and OS thread — running the same world as yours. Use me for
 real parallelism. Everything that crosses a VM boundary travels as a deep
 copy: no shared state, no identity across heaps, ever (blocks, contexts,
 classes and aliens cannot cross). All communication is asynchronous — the
 sender never blocks; replies run later as continuations.

   | w |
   w := Worker spawn: 'Worker onMessage: [:m | Worker reply: m payload]'.
   w send: #(1 2 3) onReply: [:r | Transcript show: r printString].
   w terminate.

 spawn: runs its argument once in the fresh worker, which is how its
 onMessage: handler gets installed; a handler answers with Worker reply:.
 call:on:args:onReply: is the RPC form — it performs a class-side method
 by name in the worker (no handler needed) and hands the copied result to
 your continuation; errors go to the optional onError: block. Replies are
 correlation-matched, so many requests may be in flight at once. A
 headless program waits with runLoopWhile:; a worker's death arrives as an
 ordinary message whose payload is {#workerDied. id}. In a VM that cannot
 host workers, spawn: fails cleanly with a readable error, so I am
 harmless to load anywhere."
Object subclass: Worker [
    | id |
    <classVars: Handler ReplyHandler PendingReplies NextCorr CurrentCorr CurrentPeer CocoaEventHandler UiReqHandler SnapshotHandler TranscriptLineHandler DiedHandler AnyDiedHook DeadlineSweepHook InFlightPeer InFlightCorr TickHandler DeathWatchers ExitWatchers ExitHook>

    "── spawning (primary side) ──"
    Worker class >> spawn: initSource [
        "Boot a fresh worker VM (same world as this one, via the embedder's
         registered boot closure) and run `initSource` once in it — install
         its onMessage: handler there. Answers a Worker handle."
        | i |
        i := self primSpawn: initSource.
        i isNil ifTrue: [ ^self error: 'cannot spawn a worker (no boot registered, or at the cap)' ].
        ^self new setId: i
    ]
    Worker class >> spawn [ ^self spawn: nil ]

    "SPAWN A VM THAT MAY ITSELF SPAWN (docs/process_services.md S6). Ordinary
     workers cannot — the absence is the policy that keeps a fleet a tree, and
     it stays the default. This is the deliberate exception, asked for by name:
     a demo VM whose game fans compute across the machine's cores
     (ParallelMandel) needs it, and the primary launching a demo from its own
     menu has already decided to trust it. The grant does not descend — the
     children it spawns are ordinary workers."
    Worker class >> spawnGranted: initSource [
        | i |
        i := self primSpawnGranted: initSource.
        i isNil ifTrue: [ ^self error: 'cannot spawn a worker (no boot registered, or at the cap)' ].
        ^self new setId: i
    ]
    setId: anId [ id := anId. ^self ]
    id [ ^id ]

    "── sending: always async, always a copy, never a wait ──"
    send: aPayload [
        "Fire-and-forget; any eventual reply goes to the general onReply:."
        self class primSend: id corr: 0 bytes: (Worker pickle: aPayload).
        ^self
    ]
    send: aPayload onReply: aBlock [
        "The async request pattern: send now, run the continuation when THIS
         message's reply lands (correlation-id matched). The primary never
         blocks — it sleeps until the router wakes it. The continuation is keyed
         by (originating-peer, corr) — the reply comes back FROM this worker
         `id`, so it is registered under `id` (review R4, cocoa_gui_design.md
         §7.3): two peers' independent NextCorr counters can never fire the
         wrong continuation."
        | c |
        c := Worker nextCorr.
        self class registerReply: aBlock fromPeer: id corr: c.
        self class primSend: id corr: c bytes: (Worker pickle: aPayload).
        ^self
    ]

    "── RPC: call a method by name in the worker (§5) ────────────────────
     `w call: #sieveUpTo: on: #PrimeSieve args: #(10000) onReply: [:r | …]`
     runs `PrimeSieve sieveUpTo: 10000` in the worker VM and delivers the
     (deep-copied) result to the continuation. The class must exist in the
     shared world; the selector and args pickle across as data. Both are
     async — the primary never blocks. An error (unknown class, argc
     mismatch) runs `onError:` with the message; the default logs it. A
     method that itself RAISES sends no reply, so neither block fires — v1
     has no cross-VM exception delivery (design §5)."
    call: aSelector on: aClassName args: anArray onReply: aBlock [
        ^self call: aSelector on: aClassName args: anArray
              onReply: aBlock
              onError: [ :msg | Transcript showCr: 'worker RPC error: ', msg ]
    ]
    call: aSelector on: aClassName args: anArray onReply: aBlock onError: anErrorBlock [
        ^self send: (Array with: #rpc with: aClassName with: aSelector with: anArray)
              onReply: [ :reply |
                  (reply isKindOf: Array)
                      ifFalse: [ anErrorBlock value: 'malformed RPC reply' ]
                      ifTrue: [
                          (reply at: 1) == #rpcOk
                              ifTrue: [ aBlock value: (reply at: 2) ]
                              ifFalse: [ anErrorBlock value: (reply at: 2) ] ] ]
    ]
    terminate [ self class primTerminate: id. ^self ]
    isAlive [ ^self class primAlive: id ]

    "── receiving, worker side: the host loop execs `Worker dispatchPending.`
       once per inbound message (the GameStep staging pattern) ──"
    "── the display (docs/worker_peer_links.md) ──────────────────────────
     THE ONE PEER A WORKER IS GIVEN RATHER THAN TAUGHT. Everything else it can
     reach, it can reach because that peer spoke to it first; the display is
     handed over at spawn, by the primary, because a worker whose first act is
     to draw something has to have somewhere to send it. Answers a handle, or
     nil in an embedding with no display — headless is a correct state, not a
     broken one, so callers check rather than assume."
    Worker class >> uiPeer [
        | h |
        h := self primUiPeer.
        ^h = 0 ifTrue: [ nil ] ifFalse: [ h ]
    ]

    Worker class >> hasUi ^ <Boolean> [ ^self primUiPeer ~= 0 ]

    "A handle for the display, ready to `send:`/`send:onReply:` like any other
     peer — which is the whole point of peer links: the display is not a
     special channel, it is just a worker you happen to have been introduced
     to."
    Worker class >> ui [
        | h |
        h := self uiPeer.
        ^h isNil ifTrue: [ nil ] ifFalse: [ self new setId: h ]
    ]

    "── the frame tick (a PROCESS service — docs/process_services.md S1) ──
     A VM is otherwise purely message-driven: it sleeps in its inbox and
     wakes when somebody writes to it. Right for a tool, useless for a GAME,
     which must advance whether or not anyone is talking to it. So the
     process timer service wakes us on a cadence WE ask for — one service
     thread, one wheel, and each wake arrives as an ordinary {#tick} message
     through the same door as everything else, under the same rule (one
     thing at a time, top-level, never nested). ANY VM with an inbox can
     tick — a worker, the primary — and a dead VM's timer cancels itself:
     the service checks the liveness flag before every fire.

     `Worker tickEvery: 16` is 60 Hz; 33 is 30 Hz; 0 stops it. A tick that
     raises retires the worker like any other guest fatal, and a tick block
     that runs long simply delays the next — the deadline is measured from
     the END of each tick, so a worker that cannot keep up loses rate instead
     of accumulating a debt it then pays off in a burst."
    Worker class >> tickEvery: ms [
        TickHandler isNil ifTrue: [
            Transcript showCr: 'Worker tickEvery: with no onTick: handler installed' ].
        ^self primTickEvery: ms
    ]

    Worker class >> stopTick [ ^self primTickEvery: 0 ]

    Worker class >> onTick: aBlock [ TickHandler := aBlock ]

    "Run one tick. Called by the VM when the deadline passes — never by
     application code, which should ask for a cadence and let the service
     deliver it."
    Worker class >> dispatchTick [
        TickHandler isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        ^TickHandler value
    ]

    Worker class >> onMessage: aBlock [ Handler := aBlock ]
    Worker class >> dispatchPending [
        | env payload |
        env := self primPoll.
        env isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        CurrentCorr := env at: 2.        "so reply: echoes it back"
        CurrentPeer := env at: 1.        "so reply: targets the sender (0 = primary)"
        payload := self unpickle: (env at: 3).
        "RPC (docs/multi-smalltalk-worker.md §5): an {#rpc. class. sel. args}
         payload is served directly — a worker booted with only the shared
         world answers RPCs with no onMessage: handler installed. Any other
         payload goes to the user handler as before."
        ((payload isKindOf: Array) and: [ (payload size >= 1) and: [ (payload at: 1) == #tick ] ])
            ifTrue: [ ^self dispatchTick ].
        "PLEASE STOP — recognized by EVERY worker, before any user handler.
         `Worker exit:` is a VM ending ITSELF, so somebody outside needs a way
         to ASK; without one the only outside verb was terminate, which is a
         kill. Handled centrally because a plain compute worker has no handler
         of its own to put it in, and the Monitor's `Send exit` must work on
         any row rather than only on the VMs that happen to be apps or demos.
         Those two keep their own richer arms (a window to tear down, a pane to
         close) and reach here having already tidied up."
        ((payload isKindOf: Array) and: [ (payload size >= 1) and: [ (payload at: 1) == #exitPlease ] ])
            ifTrue: [ ^self exit: (payload size >= 2 ifTrue: [ payload at: 2 ] ifFalse: [ #asked ]) ].
        (self isRpcRequest: payload) ifTrue: [ ^self serveRpc: payload ].
        Handler isNil ifFalse: [
            Handler value: (WorkerMessage from: (env at: 1) payload: payload) ]
    ]

    "── RPC service: call a method by name (§5) ──────────────────────────
     Both VMs load the same codebase, so a class the primary names, the
     worker has too. The primary sends {#rpc. targetClassName. selector.
     argsArray}; the worker resolves the class, `perform:withArguments:`,
     and replies {#rpcOk. result} — or {#rpcError. why} for an unknown
     class or an argc mismatch. A method that itself raises aborts this
     dispatch doit and sends NO reply (the primary's continuation never
     fires); v1 has no cross-VM exception delivery, by design."
    Worker class >> isRpcRequest: p [
        ^(p isKindOf: Array) and: [ (p size = 4) and: [ (p at: 1) == #rpc ] ]
    ]
    Worker class >> serveRpc: p [
        | target |
        target := self classNamed: (p at: 2).
        target isNil ifTrue: [
            ^self reply: (Array with: #rpcError
                                with: 'no such class: ', (p at: 2) printString) ].
        self reply: (Array with: #rpcOk
                           with: (target perform: (p at: 3) withArguments: (p at: 4)))
    ]
    Worker class >> classNamed: aSymbol [
        "Resolve a class by name in this worker's world, via the allClasses
         reflection sweep (34_tools.mst) — O(classes), fine for RPC."
        | want |
        want := aSymbol asString.
        ClassMirror allClasses do: [ :c | c name asString = want ifTrue: [ ^c ] ].
        ^nil
    ]
    Worker class >> reply: aPayload [
        "Answer the peer that sent the message being dispatched, echoing its
         correlation id so the sender's continuation fires. CurrentPeer is 0
         inside a worker (the primary is a worker's only peer — behavior
         unchanged); on the PRIMARY it is the originating worker (e.g. the UI
         worker for a #uiReq — CG4), so a primary-side reply: routes to the
         right terminal instead of the hardcoded primary."
        self primSend: CurrentPeer corr: CurrentCorr bytes: (self pickle: aPayload)
    ]

    Worker class >> abandonPending [
        "Drop every outstanding send:onReply: continuation and the dispatch
         context (CG4 review: recover CLEAN). The Cocoa GUI's UI worker calls
         this when its primary is restarted from source: a #uiReq that was in
         flight when the old primary died can never be answered, so its
         continuation would otherwise linger forever and leak cumulatively across
         restarts. This resets the UI worker to a truly clean idle state
         (feedback_recover_clean_or_die); the terminal simply re-issues."
        PendingReplies := Dictionary new.
        CurrentPeer := nil.
        CurrentCorr := nil ]

    "── receiving, primary side: dispatchInbox runs because the router woke
       us (or the run loop slept into a message) — never called in a poll loop ──"
    Worker class >> onReply: aBlock [ ReplyHandler := aBlock ]
    Worker class >> onWorkerDied: aBlock [
        "The supervision seam (docs/otp_workers_design.md §4.1): a 1-arg
         block handed each crashed worker's id, installed (lazily, on first
         child registration) by SupervisorRegistry. Routed shape-first in
         dispatchOne:, so once a tree exists deaths belong to it."
        DiedHandler := aBlock ]
    "PLURAL death watchers (docs/process_services.md S2) — unlike the two
     single-block hooks below, ANY number of parties may subscribe (AppVmHost
     watches its apps; a future display watches its peers) and every one is
     told every death. Fired unconditionally, before and independent of the
     supervision routing."
    Worker class >> alsoOnWorkerDied: aBlock [
        DeathWatchers isNil ifTrue: [ DeathWatchers := OrderedCollection new ].
        DeathWatchers add: aBlock.
        ^self
    ]

    "THE OTHER ENDING: a VM that chose to go (`Worker exit:`) announces itself
     before it does, and these watchers hear it — with the reason, which is
     what lets a registry say `exited (closed)` where it would otherwise have
     to guess `died`. Plural and unconditional, exactly like the death
     watchers; a 2-argument block, id and reason."
    Worker class >> alsoOnVmExiting: aBlock [
        ExitWatchers isNil ifTrue: [ ExitWatchers := OrderedCollection new ].
        ExitWatchers add: aBlock.
        ^self
    ]

    Worker class >> onAnyWorkerDied: aBlock [
        "ServiceWorker's OWN death seam (docs/otp_workers_design.md §3.4/§4.2):
         unlike DiedHandler (SupervisorRegistry's, populated only once a tree
         exists), this fires for EVERY death, supervised or not — a plain
         Worker call: against an unsupervised worker must still fail fast on
         its target's death. Installed unconditionally at world-load time
         (below, base-world), so it is always armed."
        AnyDiedHook := aBlock ]
    Worker class >> onDeadlineSweep: aBlock [
        "ServiceWorker's periodic timeout sweep (§4.2), fired every
         pumpInbox: tick REGARDLESS of whether an envelope arrived that
         tick — the sweep expires PROMISES, not messages, so it must run on
         the idle cadence too, not just when there is mail."
        DeadlineSweepHook := aBlock ]
    Worker class >> sweepIfArmed [
        DeadlineSweepHook isNil ifFalse: [ DeadlineSweepHook value ]
    ]
    "Retire a registered reply continuation WITHOUT invoking it — the
     timeout/died paths need the ORIGINAL send:onReply:/call: continuation
     to simply never fire again once ServiceWorker has already funneled
     #timeout or #workerDied to the caller's own onError:. Public (not just
     dispatchOne:'s own inline nil-out) because ServiceWorker (a different
     class) needs the identical retire — the same (peer, corr) key, the
     same nil-tombstone idiom PendingReplies already uses everywhere else."
    Worker class >> retireReplyFromPeer: peer corr: corr [
        PendingReplies isNil ifFalse: [
            PendingReplies at: (self replyKeyFor: peer corr: corr) put: nil ]
    ]
    Worker class >> onCocoaEvent: aBlock [
        "C4: the Cocoa bridge's dispatch hook — installed lazily by
         Cocoa action: (49_cocoa.mst loads after this file, so the
         reference must run at runtime, not compile time)."
        CocoaEventHandler := aBlock ]
    Worker class >> dispatchInbox [
        "Handle every queued worker→primary envelope."
        | env |
        [ (env := self primPoll) notNil ] whileTrue: [ self dispatchOne: env ]
    ]

    "── the headless event loop: sleep-until-woken, dispatch, repeat ──"
    Worker class >> runLoopWhile: conditionBlock [
        "A CLI program's main loop after kicking off its sends: sleep in the
         inbox (primAwaitInbox: blocks in the channel — the OS wake IS the
         router; zero spin), dispatch what arrived, until the condition turns
         false. The GUI never calls this — its run loop is AppKit's, fed by
         the registered wake hook. Routed entirely through pumpInbox: (below)
         so the ~250ms cadence sweeps deadlines on EVERY tick, envelope or not."
        [ conditionBlock value ] whileTrue: [ self pumpInbox: 250 ]
    ]
    Worker class >> pumpInbox: ms [
        "One iteration of a Rust-driven primary loop (Cocoa GUI CG4): sleep in
         the inbox up to `ms`, dispatch what arrived (and drain the rest), then
         RETURN to Rust — so the supervisor thread controls the cadence and can
         heartbeat/observe liveness between beats (a `runLoopWhile:` that never
         returns would give it no seam). Answers whether anything was dispatched.
         The channel send IS the wake; zero spin. Primary only.
         sweepIfArmed runs on EVERY call, envelope or not (docs/otp_workers_
         design.md §4.2) — a service-call deadline must expire on the idle
         cadence too, not only when mail happens to arrive; this is also
         where the GUI's CG4 beat (~4Hz, driving pumpInbox: directly from
         Rust) gets its own trailing sweep, with no separate timer."
        | env |
        env := self primAwaitInbox: ms.
        env isNil ifTrue: [ self sweepIfArmed. ^false ].
        self dispatchOne: env.
        self dispatchInbox.
        self sweepIfArmed.
        ^true
    ]

    Worker class >> dispatchOne: env [
        "Route one envelope. Shape-tagged control messages (worker transcript,
         Cocoa event, UI request, snapshot) are matched FIRST — before any
         correlation-id lookup — so a request whose corr coincides with an
         outstanding continuation's corr can never fire the wrong continuation
         (review R4). Then a matching pending continuation wins, keyed by
         (originating-peer, corr) so two peers' independent NextCorr counters
         cannot collide; otherwise the general reply handler. (Dictionary has no
         removeKey:, so a fired continuation is tombstoned with nil — a (peer,
         corr) key is unique, so a tombstone is never rekeyed.)"
        | peer corr k msg |
        peer := env at: 1.
        corr := env at: 2.
        "A PAYLOAD-LESS ENVELOPE IS A NUDGE, and carries nothing to dispatch.
         The boot handshake sends one to prove the primary->UI link is wired,
         and a peer INTRODUCTION (docs/worker_peer_links.md §3) is another —
         its whole content is the `from` and the reply link that Rust has
         already learned from by the time this runs. Both were documented as
         no-ops and neither was: `unpickle:` below raised `bad pickle bytes`
         on every one, which surfaced only as a drain-error line on stderr
         because nothing downstream depended on the nudge. Answering early is
         what the comments always claimed happened."
        (env at: 3) isEmpty ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        CurrentPeer := peer.        "so a primary-side reply: targets the sender"
        CurrentCorr := corr.        "so reply: echoes the inbound corr"
        msg := WorkerMessage from: peer
                            payload: (self unpickle: (env at: 3)).
        msg isWorkerTranscript ifTrue: [
            "The primary's forwarded transcript (CG4 §7.4): the UI worker routes
             it to its Transcript view via the hook; every other VM shows it on
             its own console."
            TranscriptLineHandler isNil
                ifTrue: [ Transcript show: (msg payload at: 3) ]
                ifFalse: [ TranscriptLineHandler value: (msg payload at: 3) ].
            ^self ].
        msg isCocoaEvent ifTrue: [
            CocoaEventHandler isNil ifFalse: [
                CocoaEventHandler value: (msg payload at: 2) ].
            ^self ].
        msg isWorkerDied ifTrue: [
            "Two INDEPENDENT observers, both by shape before any corr lookup
             (docs/otp_workers_design.md §4.1/§4.2): AnyDiedHook (§4.2,
             ServiceWorker's in-flight-call cross-check) fires UNCONDITIONALLY
             — it must work whether or not a supervision tree exists. Then
             DiedHandler (SupervisorRegistry's own, §4.1): installed, the
             death is consumed here; with none it FALLS THROUGH to the
             pre-existing continuation/ReplyHandler routing below — embed.rs's
             crash-isolation test observes deaths via onReply:, and
             unsupervised embeddings keep that behavior exactly."
            AnyDiedHook isNil ifFalse: [ AnyDiedHook value: (msg payload at: 2) ].
            DeathWatchers isNil ifFalse: [
                DeathWatchers do: [ :w | w value: (msg payload at: 2) ] ].
            DiedHandler isNil ifFalse: [
                DiedHandler value: (msg payload at: 2).
                ^self ] ].
        msg isVmExiting ifTrue: [
            "A DEPARTING VM'S OWN WORD, and it gets its own watchers rather
             than borrowing the death ones: an exit is a decision and a death
             is an accident, and a registry that reports them in the same
             sentence is lying about one of them. The runtime's #workerDied
             follows a moment later, as it does for every ending; a watcher
             that already retired the app on this message simply finds nothing
             left to report, which is how one ending stays one line.
             NOT CONSUMED, and that is `isWorkerDied`'s rule too: the display
             is a SHAPE-ROUTED VM whose whole handler is the reply hook, so a
             control message that returns here never reaches it — consuming
             this one stopped every app window from ever closing, which is how
             the rule got learned twice."
            ExitWatchers isNil ifFalse: [
                ExitWatchers do: [ :w |
                    w value: (msg payload at: 2)
                      value: (msg payload size >= 3
                                ifTrue: [ msg payload at: 3 ]
                                ifFalse: [ #done ]) ] ] ].
        msg isTick ifTrue: [ ^self dispatchTick ].
        msg isUiReq ifTrue: [ ^self serveUiReq: msg payload ].
        msg isSnapshot ifTrue: [
            SnapshotHandler isNil ifFalse: [ SnapshotHandler value: msg payload ].
            ^self ].
        k := (PendingReplies notNil and: [ corr ~= 0 ])
            ifTrue: [ PendingReplies at: (self replyKeyFor: peer corr: corr) ifAbsent: [ nil ] ]
            ifFalse: [ nil ].
        k isNil
            ifTrue: [ ReplyHandler isNil ifFalse: [ ReplyHandler value: msg ] ]
            ifFalse: [
                PendingReplies at: (self replyKeyFor: peer corr: corr) put: nil.
                k value: msg payload ]
    ]
    Worker class >> nextCorr [
        NextCorr isNil ifTrue: [ NextCorr := 0 ].
        NextCorr := NextCorr + 1.
        ^NextCorr
    ]

    "── (peer, corr) continuation namespacing (review R4, §7.3) ──────────
     PendingReplies is keyed by a SmallInteger packing the originating peer id
     (0 = the primary, 1..16 = a worker) with the correlation id, so peerA's
     corr=c and peerB's corr=c are DISTINCT keys and can never fire the wrong
     continuation. A packed smi is used (not a 2-element Array) because Array
     hashes by identity here — value equality without value hash would break
     Dictionary lookup; a SmallInteger hashes by value. MAX_WORKERS is 16, so
     five bits hold every peer id."
    Worker class >> replyKeyFor: peer corr: corr [
        ^(corr bitShift: 5) + peer
    ]
    Worker class >> registerReply: aBlock fromPeer: peer corr: corr [
        "GC-root a continuation under its (peer, corr) key. Shared by
         send:onReply: (replies come FROM the target worker) and uiRequest:
         (replies come FROM the primary, peer 0)."
        PendingReplies isNil ifTrue: [ PendingReplies := Dictionary new ].
        PendingReplies at: (self replyKeyFor: peer corr: corr) put: aBlock
    ]

    "── the UI worker → primary request protocol (Cocoa GUI CG4, §7.3) ────
     The UI worker (a Worker-role VM pinned to the main thread) ships a request
     to the primary (peer 0) and runs a continuation when the reply lands. The
     doit runs where the persistent objects live — on the primary — and the UI
     stays responsive because the brain is elsewhere (§6)."
    Worker class >> uiRequest: aVerb args: anArray onReply: aBlock [
        "Ship {#uiReq. corr. verb. args...} to the primary and register the
         continuation keyed by (0, corr). The reply comes back {#uiReply. corr.
         payload} FROM the primary; aBlock is handed that reply payload. The
         envelope corr IS the payload corr (one source of truth), routed by the
         existing (peer, corr) machinery."
        | c payload |
        c := self nextCorr.
        "Unwrap {#uiReply. corr. payload} → hand the continuation the payload."
        self registerReply: [ :reply | aBlock value: (reply at: 3) ] fromPeer: 0 corr: c.
        payload := (Array with: #uiReq with: c with: aVerb), anArray.
        self primSend: 0 corr: c bytes: (self pickle: payload).
        ^c
    ]
    "How many replies are still outstanding — the signal the Apple Event
     `busy` property reports (74_cocoascript.mst). Tombstoned entries (nil,
     the removeKey:-less idiom used throughout) are cancelled requests and
     do not count."
    Worker class >> pendingReplyCount [
        | n |
        PendingReplies isNil ifTrue: [ ^0 ].
        n := 0.
        PendingReplies do: [ :v | v isNil ifFalse: [ n := n + 1 ] ].
        ^n
    ]

    Worker class >> uiDoit: aSource onReply: aBlock [
        "Convenience: a Workspace ⌘P/⌘D — ship a selection as a #doit request
         and run the continuation on the (String) result."
        ^self uiRequest: #doit args: (Array with: aSource) onReply: aBlock
    ]
    Worker class >> uiRequest: aVerb args: anArray onReplyTimed: aBlock [
        "Like uiRequest:args:onReply: but hands the 2-arg block (result, ms):
         reply element 4 is the primary's eval duration (serveUiDoit:), or 0 when
         a handler answered a 3-element reply."
        | c payload |
        c := self nextCorr.
        self registerReply: [ :reply |
            aBlock value: (reply at: 3)
                   value: (reply size >= 4 ifTrue: [ reply at: 4 ] ifFalse: [ 0 ]) ]
            fromPeer: 0 corr: c.
        payload := (Array with: #uiReq with: c with: aVerb), anArray.
        self primSend: 0 corr: c bytes: (self pickle: payload).
        ^c
    ]
    Worker class >> uiDoit: aSource onReplyTimed: aBlock [
        "A ⌘P/⌘D that also reports the primary's eval time — aBlock is [:result :ms|]."
        ^self uiRequest: #doit args: (Array with: aSource) onReplyTimed: aBlock
    ]

    "── primary-side request service (§7.3): dispatch by verb. The (peer, corr)
       context is SNAPSHOTTED into locals at the top, BEFORE any request body
       runs — a doit/handler can itself dispatch and clobber CurrentPeer/
       CurrentCorr, so building the reply from the class-vars afterwards would
       route it to the wrong peer/corr (CG4 review). ──"
    Worker class >> replyTo: peer corr: corr payload: aPayload [
        "Answer a SPECIFIC (peer, corr) — for the request service, which must
         reply against a corr/peer captured before running the request body."
        self primSend: peer corr: corr bytes: (self pickle: aPayload)
    ]
    Worker class >> serveUiReq: payload [
        | verb peer corr |
        peer := CurrentPeer. corr := CurrentCorr.
        verb := payload at: 3.
        verb == #doit ifTrue: [ ^self serveUiDoit: (payload at: 4) peer: peer corr: corr ].
        verb == #doitq ifTrue: [ ^self serveUiDoitQuiet: (payload at: 4) peer: peer corr: corr ].
        verb == #refresh ifTrue: [
            ^self replyTo: peer corr: corr payload: (Array with: #uiReply with: corr
                                with: (self serveUiRefresh: (payload at: 4))) ].
        verb == #select ifTrue: [
            ^self replyTo: peer corr: corr payload: (Array with: #uiReply with: corr
                                with: (self serveUiSelect: payload)) ].
        verb == #saveMethod ifTrue: [
            ^self replyTo: peer corr: corr payload: (Array with: #uiReply with: corr
                                with: (self serveUiSaveMethod: payload)) ].
        ^self replyTo: peer corr: corr payload: (Array with: #uiReply with: corr
                            with: (Array with: #uiError with: 'unknown verb: ', verb printString))
    ]
    Worker class >> serveUiDoit: aSource peer: peer corr: corr [
        "Evaluate the selection on the primary through the existing execute_do_it
         path (prim 250, run inline under this dispatch's recovery guard) and
         reply the result's printString — a pickle-safe String the terminal
         appends. `peer`/`corr` are snapshotted by the caller BEFORE the doit runs
         (the doit may dispatch and clobber the class-vars). A raise inside the
         doit unwinds the whole dispatch and sends no reply (v1, the RPC path's
         property); the watchdog restarts the environment on a genuinely fatal
         doit (§5)."
        | result ms |
        "Time the eval on the PRIMARY (Time millisecondsToRun: → monotonic
         Smalltalk millisecondClock) and return the duration as reply element 4.
         Backward-compatible: reply at: 3 is still the result printString for the
         plain uiDoit:onReply: callers (browser accepts, liveReopen); only the
         timed uiDoit:onReplyTimed: reads element 4."
        "In-flight tracking (scripting arc): a raise inside the doit unwinds
         this whole dispatch — the reply below never sends, and before this
         arc the requester simply never heard back (the scripted `evaluate`
         hang). The supervisor's beat now recovers the error Rust-side and
         calls `recoverInFlightWith:` with the guest message; these two
         class-vars are how that call knows WHERE the lost reply was owed.
         Single-slot by design: doits are served one at a time off this
         pump (a doit that itself pumps nested uiReqs is out of scope, and
         the slot simply tracks the innermost)."
        InFlightPeer := peer. InFlightCorr := corr.
        ms := Time millisecondsToRun: [ result := self primEvalDoit: aSource ].
        InFlightPeer := nil. InFlightCorr := nil.
        ^self replyTo: peer corr: corr payload:
            (Array with: #uiReply with: corr with: result printString with: ms)
    ]

    "Called by the supervisor (supervisor.rs) on the beat AFTER a recovered
     guest error: if a doit was in flight when it raised, its requester is
     still waiting — reply the error text so a scripted `evaluate` (or a
     Workspace print-it) reports instead of hanging. The `error: ` prefix is
     OURS and unambiguous: a legitimate String result always arrives through
     `result printString` above, i.e. quoted, so an UNQUOTED `error: ` can
     only come from here (the same reasoning as the parse-error prefix,
     74_cocoascript.mst)."
    Worker class >> recoverInFlightWith: msg [
        | peer corr |
        InFlightCorr isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        peer := InFlightPeer. corr := InFlightCorr.
        InFlightPeer := nil. InFlightCorr := nil.
        ^self replyTo: peer corr: corr payload:
            (Array with: #uiReply with: corr with: 'error: ' , msg with: 0)
    ]
    "serveUiDoit:'s QUIET twin, for SCRIPTED evaluation (74_cocoascript.mst):
     identical protocol, but the eval runs with halt-on-error suppressed
     (prim 253). Halting at a raise is the right thing when a human is at
     the keyboard — the Debugger pane lights up — and a permanent hang when
     the caller is an Apple Event: the beat blocks inside the halted exec,
     so no reply, no recovery, and every later doit queues behind it
     (measured). With halt suppressed the raise unwinds to the beat's exec
     door, whose recovery arm replies `error: <message>` via
     recoverInFlightWith: — so a scripted 1/0 becomes a script error, while
     ⌘D on the same expression still opens the debugger."
    Worker class >> serveUiDoitQuiet: aSource peer: peer corr: corr [
        | result ms |
        InFlightPeer := peer. InFlightCorr := corr.
        ms := Time millisecondsToRun: [ result := self primEvalDoitQuiet: aSource ].
        InFlightPeer := nil. InFlightCorr := nil.
        ^self replyTo: peer corr: corr payload:
            (Array with: #uiReply with: corr with: result printString with: ms)
    ]
    Worker class >> primEvalDoitQuiet: aSource [
        <primitive: 264>
        ^self error: 'primEvalDoitQuiet: failed'
    ]

    "Per-view request handlers a specialized primary MAY install (checked
     first); the built-in services below are the defaults. Unknown views
     answer a benign placeholder so a stray request never raises."
    Worker class >> onUiRequest: aBlock [ UiReqHandler := aBlock ]
    Worker class >> serveUiRefresh: viewId [
        UiReqHandler isNil ifFalse: [ ^UiReqHandler value: #refresh value: viewId ].
        "#browser (CG7): the names-only class-hierarchy snapshot, produced by
         UiBrowserService (34_tools.mst) — late-bound by name so this protocol
         file carries no compile-time dependency on the tools layer."
        viewId == #browser ifTrue: [
            | svc |
            svc := self classNamed: #UiBrowserService.
            svc isNil ifFalse: [
                ^Array with: #browserTree with: svc browseSnapshot ] ].
        ^Array with: #uiPending with: viewId
    ]
    Worker class >> serveUiSelect: payload [
        UiReqHandler isNil ifFalse: [ ^UiReqHandler value: #select value: payload ].
        ^Array with: #uiPending with: (payload at: 4)
    ]
    Worker class >> serveUiSaveMethod: payload [
        UiReqHandler isNil ifFalse: [ ^UiReqHandler value: #saveMethod value: payload ].
        ^Array with: #uiPending with: #saveMethod
    ]
    "Terminal-side snapshot sink (the real view wiring is CG5); nil by default."
    Worker class >> onSnapshot: aBlock [ SnapshotHandler := aBlock ]
    "Terminal-side transcript sink: forwarded primary transcript lines (§7.4).
     The UI worker installs one that appends to its Transcript view; nil
     elsewhere (a spawned worker/plain VM shows lines on its own console)."
    Worker class >> onTranscriptLine: aBlock [ TranscriptLineHandler := aBlock ]

    Worker class >> primEvalDoit: aSource [
        "Evaluate a doit source String on THIS (primary) VM and answer the
         result oop (prim 250, CG4). On a compile/parse error the primitive
         itself answers a descriptive String; this fallback body runs only if
         the primitive is somehow absent."
        <primitive: 250>
        ^'error: doit evaluation primitive unavailable'
    ]
    Worker class >> selfId [
        "0 in the primary, i >= 1 inside worker i."
        ^self primSelfId
    ]

    "── a VM ends itself, and says so on the way out ────────────────────
     The author's rule: *a vm should exit itself and send a vm_exiting message
     as it does so*. Every other ending we had was somebody ELSE's decision
     arriving from outside — a poison envelope from the parent, a fatal error,
     a reaped epoch — and each left the peers to INFER the death from a notice
     the runtime sent afterwards. This is the other kind: the VM decides, tells
     the people who care WHILE it is still able to, and then goes.

     Order is the whole guarantee. `#vmExiting` is an ordinary message down an
     ordinary link, sent BEFORE the exit is even requested, so it is queued
     ahead of the death by construction — a peer always hears the VM's own
     goodbye before the runtime's report. And the exit itself is a message to
     this VM: the work in flight finishes first, `ensure:` blocks run, and the
     loop ends at the top, where every ending has always been handled.

     `aReason` rides along so a listener can tell a close from a finished job
     from a self-diagnosed failure; #closed is the display's."
    Worker class >> exit: aReason <Object> [
        "WHATEVER ASKED, THIS VM TIDIES UP FIRST. An exit has more than one
         route — the VM's own decision, the Monitor's `Send exit`, a window
         closing — and the teardown must not belong to any ONE of them. It was
         written into the demo client's own stop path first, and the very first
         `Send exit` from the Monitor proved that wrong: the VM left, and its
         game pane stayed on screen with nothing behind it, because the ask had
         gone straight past the cleanup.
         Nil'd BEFORE it runs, so an exit re-entered from inside the hook
         cannot loop. The hook MUST NOT RAISE — this file loads below the
         exception classes (78_exceptions.mst) and cannot catch for you; wrap
         your own body, as `DemoVmClient` does."
        ExitHook isNil ifFalse: [ | h | h := ExitHook. ExitHook := nil. h value ].
        self announceExiting: aReason.
        ^self primExitSelf
    ]

    "What this VM must do before it goes, whoever asks it to go."
    Worker class >> onExit: aBlock [ ExitHook := aBlock. ^self ]

    Worker class >> exit [ ^self exit: #done ]

    "Told to everyone this VM can reach: the display (which owns a window that
     must not outlive us) and the parent (which owns the fleet entry). Both are
     sends that may fail on a link already gone — an exit must never be
     stopped by an audience that left first."
    Worker class >> announceExiting: aReason <Object> [
        | bytes |
        bytes := self pickle: (Array with: #vmExiting with: self selfId with: aReason).
        self hasUi ifTrue: [
            self primSendQuiet: self uiPeer corr: 0 bytes: bytes ].
        self primSendQuiet: 0 corr: 0 bytes: bytes.
        ^self
    ]

    "── the numbered primitives (docs/multi-smalltalk-worker.md §5) ──"
    Worker class >> pickle: x [ <primitive: 227> ^self error: 'unpicklable (blocks/contexts/classes/aliens cannot cross a VM boundary)' ]
    Worker class >> unpickle: b [ <primitive: 228> ^self error: 'bad pickle bytes' ]
    Worker class >> primSpawn: initString [ <primitive: 220> ^nil ]
    Worker class >> primSend: i corr: c bytes: b [ <primitive: 221> ^self error: 'cannot send (unknown or dead worker)' ]
    "THE SAME SEND, MINUS THE RAISE — for the one caller that must not be
     stoppable by its audience. `announceExiting:` runs on the way out, below
     the world's exception classes (Error is defined in 78_exceptions.mst, this
     file is 47), so it can neither catch a failed send nor afford one: a peer
     that left first would otherwise abort the exit and leave the VM running
     forever, which is the exact bug the exit protocol exists to kill. Answers
     false where the raising twin would signal."
    Worker class >> primSendQuiet: i corr: c bytes: b [ <primitive: 221> ^false ]
    Worker class >> primPoll [ <primitive: 222> ^nil ]
    Worker class >> primAwaitInbox: ms [ <primitive: 223> ^nil ]
    Worker class >> primTerminate: i [ <primitive: 224> ^self ]
    Worker class >> primAlive: i [ <primitive: 225> ^false ]
    Worker class >> primSelfId [ <primitive: 226> ^0 ]
    Worker class >> primUiPeer [ <primitive: 229> ^0 ]
    Worker class >> primTickEvery: ms [ <primitive: 277> ^false ]
    Worker class >> primExitSelf [ <primitive: 278> ^false ]
    Worker class >> primSpawnGranted: initString [ <primitive: 280> ^nil ]
]
